  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nAt 1 PM CDT, skies were a mix of sun and clouds. Temperatures have \nwarmed into the 40s and 50s on 5 to 15 mph north-northwest winds.\n\nCloud cover is expected to dissipate/shift away from, at least, the \nwestern half of the CWA overnight, allowing for light \nwind/radiational cooling and temperatures falling to a point where \nfrost/freeze conditions can happen. Ensemble-powered probabilities \nsupport the need for Frost/Freeze headlines (30-90 percent\nchance of sub-freezing temperatures tonight over central and\nnorth central South Dakota), so a Freeze Warning has been \nissued for late tonight into Wednesday morning for north central\nSouth Dakota (including Brown County in northeast South \nDakota). A Frost Advisory has been issued for central South \nDakota. \n\nThe upper level closed low over the western CONUS is forecast to \nmove through the central plains during the second half of the week. \nIt's track could be close enough to bring some of the northern edge \nof deformation-zone precipitation up into the far southern portion \nof the CWA (generally south of a line from Mission Ridge to Milbank) \nWednesday night into Thursday. While that is forecast to happen, \nenergy crashing down the backside of the western CONUS upper level \nridge is forecast to sweep a strong cold front southeastward through \nthe CWA Thursday/Thursday night. It should be plenty windy behind \nthis fropa, with low level (0.5km) mixing winds progged at 35 to 40 \nknots in low level CAA. Previous events with 0.5km winds this strong \nin a setup like this have yielded advisory criteria wind gusts, \nalong with a smattering of sustained 30mph wind for an hour or more. \nEnsemble-powered probabilities support the potential for advisory \nstrength winds/gusts. A Wind Advisory may be needed for Thursday \nafternoon/night.\n\nThe degree of coldness of the low level airmass advected into the \nregion behind this cold front supports temperatures falling to or \nbelow freezing Thursday night (despite ongoing strong northwest \nwinds) and then possibly again Friday evening while transient \nsurface high pressure is centered over the border of the Dakotas and \nMinnesota.\n\nFriday through Tuesday looks dry with a warming trend forecast to \nhappen. The majority of the extended forecast is dominated mostly \nby upper level ridging across the northern plains.",
